  <h2 id="other-choices">Additional considerations when specifying a zipper</h2>
  <p>
    Beyond the number, zipper selection depends on the construction style, material, and how the closure will be used. Here are the key choices:
  </p>

  <h3 id="coil-vs-tooth">Coil vs. Tooth Zippers</h3>
  <p>
    The zipper chain can be made two ways: as interlocking plastic coils or as individual teeth.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Coil zippers:</strong> Made of woven nylon or polyester coil. Lighter, quieter, and more flexible. They compress easily and work well with soft fabrics. Great for garments, bags, and everyday products. Less visible teeth pattern.</li>
    <li><strong>Tooth (metal or plastic) zippers:</strong> Have individual teeth (metal or molded plastic). More rigid and durable. Metal teeth look cool, feel premium, and lock more positively. Molded plastic teeth offer a middle ground and can be better for heavy loads and high-stress closures.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For our work, coil zippers are versatile and forgiving. Metal tooth zippers will be your choice when you want durability and a polished look. Most of our soft-goods work uses coil zippers.  </p>

  <h3 id="separating-vs-closed">Separating vs. Closed-End Zippers</h3>
  <p>
    Zippers come in two structural types: separating (can come completely apart) and closed-end (the bottom is fixed).
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Closed-end zippers:</strong> The bottom is sewn together; when you pull the slider to the bottom, the zipper stops—both sides stay attached. Used for bags, pouches, pockets, and most closures where you don't need the zipper to fully separate. This is the default for most soft goods.</li>
    <li><strong>Separating zippers:</strong> The bottom fully opens; the left and right sides come apart completely. Used for jackets, vests, front openings, and anything that needs to come apart entirely. The two sides are independently attached to each fabric piece.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Closed-end zippers are more common and simpler to install. Separating zippers require a different construction approach and are usually reserved for outerwear or intentional front-opening garments.
  </p>

  <h3 id="locking-sliders">Locking Sliders</h3>
  <p>
    The slider is the part you grab and pull to move the teeth together and apart. Sliders come in two types:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Non-locking sliders:</strong> The zipper will slide open on its own if pulled or pushed hard enough. Fine for bags and closures that don't experience high stress during wear.</li>
    <li><strong>Locking sliders:</strong> These have a small lever or mechanism that "locks" the slider in place once it's pulled to the top. The zipper won't slide down accidentally. Essential for jackets, backpacks, and anything that needs to stay closed under movement or load.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For product prototypes, choose a locking slider if the bag or item will be handled roughly or worn with the zipper under tension. Non-locking is fine for most other products and light-use closures.
  </p>

  <h2 id="installation-and-next-steps">Installation and Next Steps</h2>
  <p>
    Now that you know how to read and specify a zipper, the next step is learning how to sew one in cleanly. Zipper installation technique makes the difference between a closure that feels premium and one that looks amateurish. Sewing zippers in is covered under <a href="/soft-goods/zipper-installation/">Zipper Installation</a>.
  </p>
